    "blogtextliquid_LBJgtB": {
      "type": "editorial--blog-text-liquid",
      "custom_css": [
        "@media screen and (min-width: 1024px) {.shopify-hardcoded-text-section-content {font-size: 24px !important; margin: 0 !important; } .shopify-hardcoded-text-section-wrapper {margin-bottom: -85px; }}",
        ".shopify-hardcoded-text-section-content {font-size: 16px; margin-left: 0px; text-align: left;}",
        "@media screen and (max-width: 1024px) {.shopify-hardcoded-text-section-wrapper {min-height: 200px; padding: 16px !important; }}"
      ],
      "name": "Blog Text Liquid",
      "settings": {
        "text_content": "<p>Somewhere in America right now, someone is unwrapping a Bath and Body Works gift set for the third year running, smiling the exact smile you give a gift that required zero information about the person receiving it. This is not anyone's fault. It is just what happens when gift shopping turns into a category to check off instead of an actual person to think about. Candles, lotion sets, a mug that says something generic about coffee: all perfectly nice, all instantly forgettable, all grabbed under pressure in the last aisle of a store that was never the plan. </p><p>Fragrance can fix this, but only if you stop shopping by budget and start shopping by who someone actually is. A 20 dollar bottle picked because it is unmistakably them will beat an 80 dollar gift set picked because it was sitting at the Sephora checkout line. Here is how to get it right this year, sorted by personality instead of price tag.</p>"
      }
    },
