    "blogtextliquid_ra7Awx": {
      "type": "editorial--blog-text-liquid",
      "custom_css": [
        "@media screen and (min-width: 1024px) {.shopify-hardcoded-text-section-content {font-size: 24px !important; margin: 0 !important; } .shopify-hardcoded-text-section-wrapper {margin-bottom: -85px; }}",
        ".shopify-hardcoded-text-section-content {font-size: 16px; margin-left: 0px; text-align: left;}",
        "@media screen and (max-width: 1024px) {.shopify-hardcoded-text-section-wrapper {min-height: 200px; padding: 16px !important; }}"
      ],
      "name": "Blog Text Liquid",
      "settings": {
        "text_content": "<p>The assumption that a bigger price automatically means a more thoughtful gift is where most people go wrong. A designer set grabbed at the airport on the way to Thanksgiving says almost nothing about the person opening it. One specific fragrance, chosen because it actually matches something true about them, says considerably more, no matter what it cost. That is the whole logic behind shopping by personality instead of price, and once it clicks, gift buying gets a lot easier. The best gift you give this year is proof you were paying attention all along, not proof you spent a lot when it counted. </p><p>This works particularly well because personality never actually mapped onto the gender split the industry likes to pretend exists. Someone with main character energy does not necessarily want the loudest bottle marketed to their gender. They<br/>want the one that actually matches the energy, regardless of which side of the store<br/>it happened to get shelved on.</p>"
      }
    },
